<edge source="middle_east_war::export_throughput" target="global_bond_stress::sovereign_spread" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.85</data>
  <data key="d36">A sustained fall in export throughput immediately reduces oil-exporting governments' FX receipts and fiscal revenues, raising near-term debt-service and rollover risk and causing investors to demand wider sovereign spreads.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">middle_east_war</data>
  <data key="d39">global_bond_stress</data>
</edge>
<edge source="middle_east_war::export_throughput" target="populist_election_wave::policy_uncertainty" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">Sudden declines in port and pipeline export capacity force governments to announce emergency interventions (export controls, subsidies, rationing) and contingency measures, increasing perceived uncertainty about near‑term economic and regulatory policy.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">middle_east_war</data>
  <data key="d39">populist_election_wave</data>
</edge>
<edge source="middle_east_war::export_throughput" target="russia_ukraine_war::energy_supply_stress" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.8</data>
  <data key="d36">A reduction in Middle East export infrastructure throughput (ports, pipelines, terminals) lowers physical export volumes available to world markets, directly increasing supply shortfalls and volatility; conversely an increase in throughput reduces energy supply stress.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">middle_east_war</data>
  <data key="d39">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="middle_east_war::market_price_level" target="middle_east_war::crude_production_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d27">intra_causal</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
</edge>
<edge source="middle_east_war::market_price_level" target="middle_east_war::global_oil_inventory" id="0">
  <data key="d27">intra_causal</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
</edge>
<edge source="middle_east_war::market_price_level" target="global_bond_stress::policy_path_expect" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.8</data>
  <data key="d36">A rise in oil and refined-product prices feeds directly into headline inflation and inflation expectations, prompting markets to re-price a higher probability of near-term monetary tightening.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">middle_east_war</data>
  <data key="d39">global_bond_stress</data>
</edge>
<edge source="middle_east_war::market_price_level" target="populist_election_wave::leading_vote_share" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.7</data>
  <data key="d36">A sharp rise in oil and fuel prices raises household living costs shortly before an election, shifting undecided and swing voters toward the leading populist candidate who promises rapid price relief and protectionist measures.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">middle_east_war</data>
  <data key="d39">populist_election_wave</data>
</edge>
<edge source="middle_east_war::market_price_level" target="russia_ukraine_war::military_conflict_lvl" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">3</data>
  <data key="d35">0.75</data>
  <data key="d36">A sustained spike in global oil prices stemming from a Middle East war raises Russian hydrocarbon revenue and budget flexibility, enabling Moscow to finance higher ammunition expenditure, logistics sustainment and contracted supplies that translate into increased conventional operations intensity in Ukraine.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
